  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nOvernight Saturday, a strengthening low level jet is expected to \ndrive nocturnal thunderstorm development just to our northwest, and \nthese storms may skirt our portion of northeast Missouri as they \nmove slowly southeast through Sunday morning. It is unclear how far \ninto our area these storms will make it before diminishing, but in \nany case their outflow should help nudge the stalled cold front \nslowly southward during the day Sunday. As this boundary slowly \nmoves south between Sunday morning and Monday night, additional \nrounds of showers and storms are expected to develop, although the \ntiming and placement of each round is very uncertain. \nPrecipitation probabilities during this window remain rather \nhigh (60-80%) in most areas and appear to be slightly favored \nSunday night/Monday morning, but confidence in the specific \ntiming is very low. While it is too soon to pinpoint timing and \nplacement, most areas are favored (60+%) to see at least some \nrain during this two day window, with a reasonable chance \n(40-70%) to see a beneficial soaking (1/2 inch or more).\n\nMeanwhile, temperatures behind the advancing front continue to trend \ncooler and cooler in latest ensemble guidance, and it is becoming \nmore and more clear that significant heat relief is on the way. This \nrelief is first expected in our far north Sunday, although the \nremainder of the area is expected to remain in the 90s for one more \nday (albeit with lower humidity). By Monday, this front is expected \nto sink well into Missouri, and latest trends continue to increase \nits speed. Still, forecast deterministic temperatures continue to \nexhibit a 20-25 degree range across the area Monday, and ensemble \ntemperature spreads remain high due to timing uncertainty of the \nfront. By Tuesday though these uncertainties disappear as the front \nis expected to clear well to the south, with latest ensemble mean \ntemperatures only in the 60s and 70s area-wide along with much \nnarrower ensemble spreads.",
